    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436654
    m_Localized: "MASTERY UP: {0} → {1}"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436655
    m_Localized: "TONE-DEAF"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436656
    m_Localized: "ON BEAT (SOMETIMES)"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436657
    m_Localized: "LEGIT MUSICIAN"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436658
    m_Localized: "SHARP EAR"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436659
    m_Localized: "Johann Sebastian Bach once walked some 400 km just to hear an organist play, and was briefly jailed by a duke for trying to quit his job. He fathered twenty children and still found time for more than a thousand works. Counterpoint is his combo system: every voice attacks at once."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436660
    m_Localized: "Ludwig van Beethoven kept writing masterpieces after going almost completely deaf, which says a lot about stubbornness. He moved house in Vienna dozens of times and was famous for his temper. Expect sudden dynamics: quiet, then a sforzando to the face."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436661
    m_Localized: "Johannes Brahms played piano in Hamburg's harbour taverns as a teenager before Robert Schumann hailed him as the next big thing. He then spent about twenty years on his First Symphony, terrified of Beethoven's shadow. He also wrote the world's most famous lullaby, so don't fall asleep mid-round."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436662
    m_Localized: "Frédéric Chopin wrote almost only for the piano and preferred intimate salons to big concert halls. Born in Poland and settled in Paris, he asked for his heart to go back home, and it rests in a Warsaw church. His rubato bends time; so do his dodges."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436663
    m_Localized: "George Frideric Handel was born in Germany and conquered London with operas and oratorios. He wrote Messiah in roughly three and a half weeks, and as a young man survived a sword duel thanks, the story goes, to a coat button. Here, Hallelujah is not a celebration: it is a warning."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436664
    m_Localized: "Joseph Haydn wrote over a hundred symphonies and earned the nickname 'Father of the Symphony'. In his Farewell Symphony the musicians leave the stage one by one, a polite hint to his prince that everyone wanted a holiday. His Surprise Symphony proves he also enjoys a sudden hit."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436665
    m_Localized: "Claudio Monteverdi helped invent opera: his L'Orfeo, from 1607, is still staged today. He later ran the music at St Mark's in Venice for three decades, bridging the Renaissance and the Baroque. He has been making dramatic entrances for over four hundred years."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436666
    m_Localized: "Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart toured Europe's courts as a child prodigy and wrote more than 600 works before dying at just 35. His letters are full of very rude jokes, so the elegance is partly a costume. Fast, light and precise: he finishes a combo before you notice it started."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436667
    m_Localized: "Johann Pachelbel was a respected German organist who taught Johann Christoph Bach, J. S. Bach's older brother. His Canon in D slept for centuries before becoming a 20th-century wedding obsession. The same eight bass notes, over and over: patience is his weapon."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436668
    m_Localized: "Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky worked as a clerk at Russia's Ministry of Justice before committing fully to music. He went on to write Swan Lake, The Nutcracker and an overture that calls for real cannons. He conducted at the opening of Carnegie Hall in 1891, so big crowds do not scare him."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436669
    m_Localized: "Antonio Vivaldi, nicknamed 'the Red Priest' for his hair, taught music to the girls of Venice's Ospedale della Pietà. He wrote around five hundred concertos and attached sonnets to The Four Seasons describing storms, birds and frozen roads. He died poor in Vienna, then became one of the most played composers on Earth."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436670
    m_Localized: "Richard Wagner wrote The Ring of the Nibelung, an opera cycle of about fifteen hours spread over four evenings. He built his own theatre in Bayreuth to stage it, after years of dodging creditors and a warrant from the 1849 Dresden uprising. Nothing he does is short, this fight included."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436671
    m_Localized: "Defeat any version of this composer to unlock their story."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2467484298436672
    m_Localized: "MYSTERY"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
